  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Becoming noticeably less humid through the day \ntoday with cooler air settling in for tonight.\n\nWith the cold front now pushing into the southern tier, high \nclouds are peeling off to the south. However, moisture remains \ntrapped in the boundary layer resulting in widespread stratus \nand a few areas of fog. Dewpoints range from the upper 60s to \nthe low 70s in these predawn hours. Showers are expected to \nremain isolated at best. A north breeze with gusts up to 20 mph \nwill advect a cooler and drier air mass, with temps and dewpts \nin the 40s for central PA by late tonight. Patchy valley fog is \npossible early Saturday morning. Most, if not all of Saturday \nwill be dry.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rain returning by Sunday, heavy at times. \n\nPeriods of potentially heavy rainfall are expected Saturday \nnight into Sunday. Both NAEFS and ECMWF SATs show at least 90th \npercentile PWs returning to the region, which is supportive of\nat least a marginal excessive rain signal. A surface low \nexpected to track over the Great Lakes will usher in this \nmoisture and more than enough forcing for widespread \nprecipitation. A few to several hundred J/kg of MUCAPE should \nsupport some heavier downpours, but little to no CAPE above the\n-10C level suggests the lightning threat will be limited. Most\n likely rainfall amounts with the Sunday system are 0.50-1.50\".\n \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Rainy pattern continues into much of next week. \n\nPoPs are progged to drop into the 30-40 pct range on Monday as \nhigh pressure wedges in from the north. However, the jet stream \nwill remain active and amplified with Pacific disturbances \nmoving across the nation. By Tuesday, most guidance has a slow \nmoving trough in the Ohio Valley advecting warm air above a \nwedge of cool sfc air in place from the aforementioned high \npressure system. This setup may linger for a few days, with up \nto several inches of rainfall possible through the week. The \ncold air damming will also result in high temps only in the 50s \nto low 60s.",
